WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.920
This is Bruges, in Belgium,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.640
famous for chocolate, lace and beer.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.499
And this is the Half Moon Brewery.

00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:09.610
There's been a brewery on this site for somewhere
around five centuries.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.460
There used to be 30 in the city, but modern
production

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.589
and making enough beer to meet the export
demands of the world

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:16.980
make that difficult.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.250
The Half Moon Brewery moved their bottling
operation outside the city years ago,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.880
about two miles, but brewing stayed here.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.450
This was the last brewery in Bruges.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.580
There have been new brewers coming along with
new ideas recently,

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.809
so now we are not the only one any more,

00:00:32.809 --> 00:00:35.410
but we're definitely the oldest one.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.420
Who are we to change the location of this
brewery?

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.600
Logistics are tricky when your entire city
centre is a World Heritage Site.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.429
Getting ingredients in was tough enough,

00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:47.899
but having to also bring tanker trucks through
narrow cobbled streets,

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:51.239
fill them up with beer and then drive them
out to the bottling plant two miles away,

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:53.649
well, they needed a better solution.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:54.710
So they built one.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.899
The beer gets into the pipe right from the
brewing room, which is here,

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:01.519
and goes from one tank here in the city centre
of Bruges

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:04.470
to another tank on the other side in the bottling
plant.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.850
The pipeline's about this size, that's the
outer diameter,

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.220
and inside that one you have four pipes which
are approximately 8cm diameter.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.480
The beer goes through at the rate of about
5,000-6,000l/hr.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.020
It's not such a high flow rate that it's under
pressure, it's relatively slow.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.720
So it takes a batch of beer about a small
hour to get to the other side.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.660
The beer enters our site here, it goes in
one of the big tanks,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.180
the lagering process goes on for three weeks,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.410
then the beer has to be filtered, pasteurised,
we add sugar and yeast,

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.780
then everything goes into the bottle.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.090
Yeast inside the bottle has to do the second
fermentation,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.490
and that's happening in a warm room.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.550
After the transport, we clean our pipes with
cleaning products,

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.000
we put disinfection inside of the pipe,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.520
then we flush it with around 300 litres
of water

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.170
before we put beer into the pipeline again.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.550
Outside the city centre, along highways,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.180
sure, regular excavators were fine.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.629
But here, some more creative approaches were
needed.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:13.810
Getting permission for this project wasn't
easy,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.850
but we were lucky to have some forward-thinking
people in our city

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.810
that agreed with this idea and gave us the
permit.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.260
It runs only under public domain, so only
under the streets,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.239
not under houses or private properties,

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:31.019
and they actually drill from a distance,

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:34.049
so they make a hole on every corner of the
street,

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:39.189
and then they have a guided drilling whereby
they don't need to open up

00:02:39.189 --> 00:02:41.129
all the streets in order to drill.

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:43.959
Four months of construction isn't normally
a project that's welcomed,

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:47.640
but the locals of Bruges seemed to think that
this was something special.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.590
The locals initially thought it was a joke,
I think.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.349
People really took to the idea and actually
joked a lot about

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:59.239
tapping off the pipeline and getting beer
in their own home or kitchen.

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:05.400
It wouldn't be possible to connect a pub or
even a house along the pipeline,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.409
however, that's why we invented the crowdfunding,

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:10.329
that's where the idea came from,

00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:14.180
so people could crowdfund to actually get
some beer for life,

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.569
which was the replacement of having a little
beer tap in their own house!

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:20.709
Thank you to everyone at the Half Moon Brewery
in Bruges.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:21.430
Pull down the description for more about them!

